{{Main|2016 Delaware gubernatorial election}}
{{see also|2016 Delaware lieutenant gubernatorial election}}
Two-term incumbent [[Governor of Delaware|Governor]] [[Jack Markell]] was term-limited in 2016.<ref>DE Const. art. III, §&nbsp;5</ref> Former Democratic [[Delaware Attorney General]] [[Beau Biden]], the son of [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] [[Joe Biden]], announced his intention to run and was seen as the front-runner in the Democratic primary and general election, but he died of brain cancer at the age of 46 on May 30, 2015.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.weeklystandard.com/blogs/joe-biden-announces-passing-son-beau-biden_960494.html |title=Joe Biden Announces Passing of Son, Beau Biden |work=The Weekly Standard |author=Daniel Harper |date=May 30, 2015 |access-date=May 31, 2015 |archive-date=May 31, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150531103317/http://www.weeklystandard.com/blogs/joe-biden-announces-passing-son-beau-biden_960494.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Beau Biden says he plans to run for governor in Delaware|url=http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2014/04/17/beau-biden-says-he-plans-to-run-for-governor-in-delaware/?hpt=hp_t2|access-date=April 17, 2014|newspaper=CNN|date=April 17, 2014|archive-date=January 18, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160118132828/http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2014/04/17/beau-biden-says-he-plans-to-run-for-governor-in-delaware/?hpt=hp_t2|url-status=dead}}</ref> Representative [[John Carney (Delaware politician)|John Carney]], a former [[Lieutenant Governor of Delaware]] who also ran for governor [[Delaware gubernatorial election, 2008|in 2008]], won the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nationaljournal.com/s/73082/rep-john-carney-run-governor-delaware|title=Rep. John Carney to Run for Governor of Delaware|work=[[National Journal]]|last1=Bruggeman|first1=Karyn|last2=Railey|first2=Kimberly|date=September 16, 2015|access-date=September 16, 2015}}</ref> State senator [[Colin Bonini]] won the Republican nomination.

Carney won the election, taking 58.3% of the vote compared to Bonini's 39.2%.<ref name="politicoresults">{{cite news|title=2016 Governor Election Results|url=https://www.politico.com/2016-election/results/map/governor/|access-date=7 March 2017|agency=AP|publisher=Politico|date=13 December 2016}}</ref>

