{{Main|2016 Indiana gubernatorial election}}
One-term incumbent [[Governor of Indiana|Governor]] [[Mike Pence]] announced his bid for re-election. Pence won in [[Indiana gubernatorial election, 2012|2012]] with 49.6% of the vote. Pence previously served as a U.S. Representative from 2001 to 2013 and was Chairman of the [[House Republican Conference]] from 2009 to 2011. Pence had [[Republican Party presidential candidates, 2016#Publicly expressed interest|expressed interest]] in running for [[President of the United States]] in the [[2016 United States presidential election|2016 presidential election]], but declined. However, Pence withdrew his bid for a second term on July 15, 2016, to run for vice president as running mate to [[Donald Trump]].<ref name="nbcnews.com">{{cite web|url=http://www.nbcnews.com/storyline/2016-conventions/its-official-trump-announces-mike-pence-vp-pick-n610111|title=It's Official: Trump Announces Pence as VP Pick|work=nbcnews.com|access-date=August 2, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/realDonaldTrump/status/753965070003109888|title=Donald J. Trump on Twitter|work=twitter.com|access-date=August 2, 2016}}</ref> Pence was replaced as the gubernatorial nominee by Lieutenant Governor [[Eric Holcomb]].

The 2012 Democratic nominee, former State House Speaker [[John R. Gregg]], won the Democratic nomination.<ref name=greggannounce>{{cite web | url=http://www.indystar.com/story/news/politics/2015/04/30/gregg-set-to-announce-governor-bid-pence-rematch-likely/26626999/|title=Source: Democrat John Gregg set to announce governor bid|work=[[The Indianapolis Star]]|last=LoBianco|first=Tom|date=April 30, 2015|access-date=April 30, 2015}}</ref> State Representative Karen Tallian and [[Indiana Superintendent of Public Instruction]] [[Glenda Ritz]] both withdrew their candidacies.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.indystar.com/story/news/politics/2015/08/07/glenda-ritz-drops-out--governors-race/31307459/|title=Glenda Ritz drops out of governor's race|work=[[The Indianapolis Star]]|last1=Cook|first1=Tony|last2=Schneider|first2=Chelsea|date=August 7, 2015|access-date=August 7, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nwitimes.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/elections/tallian-drops-out-of-governor-s-race/article_73b5ffef-4ce1-536c-9825-568ca5d660a4.html|title=Tallian drops out of governor's race|work=[[The Times of Northwest Indiana]]|last=Carden|first=Dan|date=August 17, 2015|access-date=August 17, 2015}}</ref> State Representative [[Terri Austin]], [[South Bend, Indiana|South Bend]] Mayor [[Peter Buttigieg]], former [[Lieutenant Governor of Indiana|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Kathy Davis]], [[Kokomo, Indiana|Kokomo]] Mayor Greg Goodnight, [[Lafayette, Indiana|Lafayette]] Mayor Tony Roswarski, and House Minority Leader [[Scott Pelath]] declined to run for governor. Potential Democratic candidates include former [[United States Attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the Southern District of Indiana|Southern District of Indiana]] and former [[Secretary of State of Indiana]] [[Joe Hogsett]], President and CEO of the Biocrossroads Initiative and nominee for the U.S. Senate [[United States Senate election in Indiana, 2000|in 2000]] David Johnson, [[Hammond, Indiana|Hammond]] Mayor [[Thomas McDermott, Jr.]], physician, former Commissioner for the Indiana State Department of Health and candidate for [[Indiana's 7th congressional district]] in [[United States House of Representatives elections in Indiana, 2008#District 7|2008]], Woody Myers, former [[Indiana Senate|State Senate]] Minority Leader and nominee for [[Lieutenant Governor of Indiana|lieutenant governor]] in [[Indiana gubernatorial election, 2012|2012]] [[Vi Simpson]], U.S. Representative [[Pete Visclosky]] and former [[Evansville, Indiana|Evansville]] Mayor [[Jonathan Weinzapfel]].<ref name=INFT /><ref name=greggdrops>{{cite web | url=http://www.theindychannel.com/news/local-news/dem-john-gregg-drops-out-of-2016-indiana-governors-race | title=Dem. John Gregg drops out of 2016 Indiana governor's race | work=The Indy Channel | date=October 23, 2013 | access-date=October 24, 2013 | author=Norman Cox | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131029235741/http://www.theindychannel.com/news/local-news/dem-john-gregg-drops-out-of-2016-indiana-governors-race | archive-date=October 29, 2013 | url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name=bayhnot>{{cite news|url=http://www.nwitimes.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/bayh-not-running-for-governor-in/article_f1c06cff-259c-5fa6-a3cb-1fe418e3fe62.html |title=Bayh not running for governor in 2016|publisher=nwi.com|date=September 13, 2014|access-date=October 26, 2014}}</ref><ref name=gauging>{{cite news |url=http://howeypolitics.com/Content/Columns/David-Kitchell/Article/Dave-Kitchell--Gauging-a-potential-2016-Democrat-ticket/10/22/10561 |title=Dave Kitchell: Gauging a potential 2016 Democrat ticket |publisher=Howey Politics Indiana |date=November 4, 2013 |access-date=October 26, 2014 |archive-date=October 26, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141026171103/http://howeypolitics.com/Content/Columns/David-Kitchell/Article/Dave-Kitchell--Gauging-a-potential-2016-Democrat-ticket/10/22/10561 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name=searching>{{cite news|url=http://www.indystar.com/story/opinion/columnists/russ-pulliam/2014/02/21/indiana-democrats-searching-for-gubernatorial-candidates/5691961/ |title=Indiana Democrats searching for gubernatorial candidates |publisher=The Indianapolis Star |date=February 21, 2014 |access-date=October 26, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Evan Bayh won't run in 2016; Gregg, McDermott assess|url=http://www.howeypolitics.com/Content/Default/Lead-Story/Article/Evan-Bayh-won-t-seek-the-governorship-in-2016/-3/346/11786|access-date=September 12, 2014|publisher=Howey Politics|date=September 15, 2014|archive-date=September 15, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140915162821/http://www.howeypolitics.com/Content/Default/Lead-Story/Article/Evan-Bayh-won-t-seek-the-governorship-in-2016/-3/346/11786|url-status=dead}}</ref> Former Governor and Senator [[Evan Bayh]] had considered running,<ref name=INFT>{{cite news|last=Livingston|first=Abby|title=Ambitious Hoosiers Wait for Future Statewide Races {{!}} Farm Team|url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/ambitious_hoosiers_wait_for_future_statewide_races_farm_team-225788-1.html|access-date=April 3, 2014|newspaper=Roll Call|date=June 19, 2013}}</ref> but has since announced he is running for the [[United States Senate election in Indiana, 2016|U.S. Senate]] in 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.indystar.com/story/news/politics/2016/07/13/bayh-makes-senate-bid-official/87026058/|title=Evan Bayh on running for Senate, Indiana residency|work=The Indianapolis Star |access-date=August 2, 2016}}</ref>

Holcomb won election with 51.4% of the vote, while Gregg took 45.4%.<ref name="politicoresults"/>

