Two-term incumbent [[Governor of Missouri|Governor]] [[Jay Nixon]] was term-limited in 2016.<ref name="Nixon term limits">{{cite news|last=McClellan|first=Bill|title=Bill McClellan: Jay Nixon could make presidential bid in 2016|url=http://www.stltoday.com/news/local/columns/bill-mcclellan/bill-mcclellan-jay-nixon-could-make-presidential-bid-in/article_21c02e01-1968-5a2e-8758-f6d0f2405cb3.html|access-date=March 3, 2013|newspaper=St. Louis Post Dispatch|date=May 10, 2012}}</ref> U.S. Senator and [[Missouri gubernatorial election, 2004|2004]] gubernatorial nominee [[Claire McCaskill]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://politicmo.com/2015/01/12/claire-mccaskill-governor-koster/|work=PoliticMo|title=Claire McCaskill will not run for governor in 2016|date=January 12, 2015|access-date=January 12, 2015|last=Yokley|first=Eli}}</ref> and [[State Treasurer of Missouri|State Treasurer]] [[Clint Zweifel]] declined to run for governor.<ref>{{cite news|last=Rosenbaum|first=Jason|title=Zweifel decides against 2016 bid for governor|url=https://www.stlbeacon.org/#!/content/30266/zweifel_governor_koster_departure|access-date=April 10, 2013|newspaper=St. Louis Beacon|date=April 9, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130720014459/https://www.stlbeacon.org/#!/content/30266/zweifel_governor_koster_departure|archive-date=July 20, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> On August 3, 2016, [[Missouri Attorney General]] [[Chris Koster]] won the nomination with a dominating 79% of the primary vote.<ref name="auto">{{cite web|last1=Martellaro|first1=Alexandria|title=Greitens, Koster win gubernatorial primaries|url=http://www.ksdk.com/news/politics/koster-greitens-win-party-nominations-for-governor/286681986|website=KSDK|publisher=KSDK News|access-date=17 August 2016|archive-date=August 22, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160822180410/http://www.ksdk.com/news/politics/koster-greitens-win-party-nominations-for-governor/286681986|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Former [[Speaker of the Missouri House of Representatives|Speaker]] of the [[Missouri House of Representatives]] [[Catherine Hanaway]], businessman John Brunner, State Senator [[Bob Dixon (Missouri politician)|Bob Dixon]], former Navy SEAL [[Eric Greitens]], and [[Lieutenant Governor of Missouri|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Peter Kinder]] ran for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.kansascity.com/news/government-politics/article36693603.html|title=Former Navy SEAL Greitens running as Republican for Missouri governor|work=[[The Kansas City Star]]|last1=Scher Zagier|first1=Alan|last2=Ballentine|first2=Summer|date=September 26, 2015|access-date=September 28, 2015}}</ref> State Representative Bart Korman and U.S. Representative [[Blaine Luetkemeyer]]<ref>{{cite news|author=Giacomo Bologna|title=Show Me Nothing: Missouri Members Entrenched|url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/show_me_nothing_missouri_members_entrenched-228101-1.html|newspaper=Roll Call|date=October 2, 2013|access-date=October 3, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://moscout.com/greitens-for-gov/ |title=Greitens for Gov? |publisher=Missouri Scout |date=July 8, 2014 |access-date=November 25, 2014 |archive-date=October 21, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021235042/http://moscout.com/greitens-for-gov/ |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://themissouritimes.com/15842/bart-korman-running-governor/|title=Is Bart Korman running for governor?|newspaper=The Missouri Times|last=Wingo|first=Kelsey|date=January 13, 2015|access-date=January 28, 2015}}</ref> declined to run for governor. [[State Auditor of Missouri|Missouri State Auditor]] [[Tom Schweich]] had been a candidate for governor before he committed suicide in February 2015.<ref>{{cite web|author=Joe Millitzer|title=Missouri Auditor Schweich dies after self-inflicted gunshot wound|url=http://fox2now.com/2015/02/26/missouri-auditor-schweich-hospitalized-for-medical-problem/|publisher=[[KTVI]] Fox 2 Now|date=February 26, 2015}}</ref> On August 3, 2016, Greitens won the nomination with 35% of the vote.<ref name="auto" />

Greitens won the election, taking 51.3% of the vote compared to Koster's 45.4%.<ref name="politicoresults"/>

