{{Main|2016 New Hampshire gubernatorial election}}
Two-term Democratic incumbent [[Governor of New Hampshire|Governor]] [[Maggie Hassan]] ran for the [[United States Senate election in New Hampshire, 2016|U.S. Senate]], narrowly defeating incumbent Republican [[Kelly Ayotte]], instead of running for a third term as governor. She won a second term in [[New Hampshire gubernatorial election, 2014|2014]] with 53% of the vote against Republican businessman [[Walt Havenstein]]. [[Executive Council of New Hampshire|Executive Councilor]] [[Colin Van Ostern]] defeated Deputy Secretary of State and Director of Securities Regulation [[Mark Connolly (public official)|Mark Connolly]] for the Democratic nomination.

Executive Councilor [[Chris Sununu]], state representative and entrepreneur [[Frank Edelblut]], and Jon Lavoie ran for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nh1.com/news/chris-sununu-announces-run-for-nh-governor/|title=Chris Sununu announces run for NH Governor|publisher=NH1.com|date=January 14, 2015|access-date=September 7, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150919153750/http://www.nh1.com/news/chris-sununu-announces-run-for-nh-governor/|archive-date=September 19, 2015|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Sununu defeated his challengers for the Republican nomination.

Despite most pre-election polling suggesting a Democratic win, Sununu narrowly won election with 49% of the vote. Van Ostern won 46.7% and Libertarian [[Max Abramson]] won 4.3% of the vote.<ref name="politicoresults"/>

