{{Main|2016 North Dakota gubernatorial election}}
One-term incumbent [[Governor of North Dakota|Governor]] [[Jack Dalrymple]] declined to seek re-election.<ref>{{cite news|last=Scheyder|first=Ernest|title=North Dakota's governor says will not seek re-election in 2016|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-north-dakota-governor-idUSKCN0QT20B20150824|access-date=August 24, 2015|newspaper=Reuters}}</ref> Dalrymple was elected to his first full term with 63.1% of the vote in [[North Dakota gubernatorial election, 2012|2012]], after first taking the seat in 2010 after [[John Hoeven]] resigned to become a U.S. Senator. Dalrymple was previously [[Lieutenant Governor of North Dakota]] from 2000 to 2010.

Republican candidates included Attorney General [[Wayne Stenehjem]],<ref name="Governing Predictions">{{cite news|last1=Jacobson|first1=Louis|title=Democrats Have More Seats to Defend in 2015–2016 Governors Races|url=http://www.governing.com/topics/politics/gov-democrats-seats-defend-2015-2016-governors-races.html|access-date=January 27, 2015|publisher=Governing Magazine|date=January 23, 2015}} Governing Magazine uses a scale of "vulnerable", "potentially vulnerable", and "safe."</ref> businessman [[Doug Burgum]],<ref>{{cite news|last=Springer|first=Patrick|title=Doug Burgum announces bid for North Dakota governor|url=http://www.grandforksherald.com/news/politics/3924505-doug-burgum-announces-bid-north-dakota-governor|access-date=May 21, 2016|newspaper=Grand Forks Herald|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304113836/http://www.grandforksherald.com/news/politics/3924505-doug-burgum-announces-bid-north-dakota-governor|archive-date=March 4, 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> and State Representative and plastic surgeon [[Rick Becker]]. Burgum won the nomination.

Potential Democratic candidates included former Congressman [[Earl Pomeroy]], state Senator [[George B. Sinner]] and state Senate Minority Leader [[Mac Schneider]].<ref name="Governing Predictions" /> Former Agriculture Commissioner [[Sarah Vogel]] formed an exploratory a campaign but announced on Jan. 28, 2016 that she will not run for governor. Senator [[Heidi Heitkamp]] declined to run for governor.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.inforum.com/news/3835235-heitkamp-says-she-wont-run-governor-2016/ |title=Heitkamp says she won't run for governor in 2016|publisher=In Forum |date=September 9, 2015 |access-date=September 9, 2015}}</ref> State representative [[Marvin Nelson]] won his party's nomination.

Burgum won the election, taking 76.7% of the vote, while Nelson won 19.4%.<ref name="politicoresults"/>

