{{Main|2016 Oregon gubernatorial special election}}
[[Governor of Oregon|Governor]] [[John Kitzhaber]], who won reelection in 2014 with 49.9% of the vote, announced his pending resignation on February 13, 2015, amid controversy surrounding [[Cylvia Hayes|his fiancée's]] consulting contracts and work within his administration.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/govbeat/wp/2015/02/13/oregons-new-governor-helped-usher-kitzhaber-out/|title=Oregon's new governor helped usher Kitzhaber out|work=The Washington Post|first=Reid|last=Wilson|date=February 13, 2015|access-date=February 13, 2015}}</ref> [[Kate Brown]], Oregon's Secretary of State, was sworn in as governor on February 18, 2015, upon Kitzhaber's resignation. In accordance with the [[Constitution of Oregon]], a special election was held in 2016 for the remainder of the term to which Kitzhaber was elected in [[Oregon gubernatorial election, 2014|2014]]. Brown ran against Republican [[Bud Pierce]], an Oncologist from Salem.<ref>Theriault, Denis C. (September 18, 2015) [http://www.oregonlive.com/politics/index.ssf/2015/09/kate_brown_lets_it_buck_in_pen.html "Kate Brown makes clear she's running for governor"], ''[[The Oregonian]]''. Retrieved September 18, 2015.</ref>

Brown won the election, taking 50.5% of the vote compared to Pierce's 43.8%.<ref name="politicoresults"/> In winning, Kate Brown became the first openly [[LGBTQ]] Governor elected in the United States.<ref>{{cite web|last=Johnson |first=Chris |url=https://www.washingtonblade.com/2016/11/08/kate-brown-becomes-first-openly-lgbt-person-elected-governor/ |title=Kate Brown becomes first openly LGBT person elected governor |publisher=Washingtonblade.com |date=2016-11-08 |access-date=2017-08-25}}</ref>

