{{Main|2016 Vermont gubernatorial election}}
Three-term incumbent [[Governor of Vermont|Governor]] [[Peter Shumlin]] declined to seek re-election.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/governor-races/244301-vermont-governor-will-not-seek-reelection/|title=Vermont governor will not seek reelection |work=The Hill |author=Ben Kamisar |date=June 8, 2015 |access-date=June 8, 2015}}</ref> He was re-elected with 46.4% of the vote in [[Vermont gubernatorial election, 2014|2014]]. As he did not receive a majority of the vote, the [[Vermont General Assembly]] was required to choose the winner. The Vermont Assembly chose Shumlin over Republican nominee [[Scott Milne]] by 110 votes to 69.<ref name=shumlinbeatsmilne>{{cite news|url=http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/2015/01/08/vermont-legislature-vote/21394309/|title=Shumlin defeats Milne in Legislature governor vote|author=Free Press Staff|date=January 8, 2015|access-date=January 8, 2015}}</ref>

[[Sue Minter]] defeated former state senator [[Matt Dunne]] for the Democratic nomination for governor.<ref name=thallenbeck /> House Speaker [[Shap Smith]] withdrew from the race. Former lieutenant governor [[Doug Racine]] declined to run for governor.<ref name="Circus">April Burbank, Paris Achen & Mike Donoghue, [http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/2015/06/08/shumlin-decided-against-2016-re-election/28690591/ Shumlin's decision kicks off political 'circus'], ''Burlington Free Press'' (June 8, 2015).</ref><ref name="Galloway">Anne Galloway, [http://vtdigger.org/2015/06/08/crowded-field-likely-to-line-up-for-open-governors-seat/ Crowded field likely to line up for open governor's seat], Vtdigger (June 8, 2015).</ref>

Lieutenant Governor [[Phil Scott]] won the Republican nomination.<ref name=thallenbeck>{{cite web|url=http://www.sevendaysvt.com/OffMessage/archives/2015/09/08/scott-pins-gubernatorial-campaign-on-fiscal-responsibility|title=Scott Pins Gubernatorial Campaign on 'Fiscal Responsibility'|work=sevendaysvt.com|first=Terri|last=Hallenbeck|date=September 8, 2015|access-date=September 8, 2015}}</ref> Former state senator and former [[Vermont Auditor of Accounts]] [[Randy Brock]] and 2014 Republican nominee [[Scott Milne]] declined to run for governor. Former Libertarian gubernatorial candidate Dan Feliciano was a potential candidate.<ref name="Circus" /><ref name="Galloway" />

Scott won the election, taking 52.9% compared to Minter's 44.2%.<ref name="politicoresults"/>

