{{Main|2016 West Virginia gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of West Virginia|Governor]] [[Earl Ray Tomblin]] was term-limited in 2016.<ref name=TomblinTermLimits>{{cite news|last=King|first=Joselyn|title=Tomblin, Maloney win nominations|url=http://wetzelchronicle.com/page/content.detail/id/504690/Tomblin--Maloney-Win-Nominations.html?nav=5001|access-date=March 3, 2013|newspaper=Wetzel Chronicle|date=May 18, 2011}}</ref> Tomblin was first elected in a [[West Virginia gubernatorial special election, 2011|2011 special election]] after [[Joe Manchin]] resigned after being [[United States Senate special election in West Virginia, 2010|elected to]] the [[United States Senate]]. Tomblin then won election to a full term in [[West Virginia gubernatorial election, 2012|2012]].

Democratic candidates included former U.S. Attorney [[Booth Goodwin]], state Senator [[Jeff Kessler]], and businessman [[Jim Justice]]. Former Senator [[Carte Goodwin]], former [[List of Speakers of the West Virginia House of Delegates|Speaker]] of the [[West Virginia House of Delegates]] [[Rick Thompson]], West Virginia [[State Treasurer]] [[John Perdue]], State Senator [[Mike Green (West Virginia politician)|Mike Green]] and State Delegates [[Doug Reynolds (West Virginia)|Doug Reynolds]], [[Doug Skaff]] and [[West Virginia Secretary of State]] [[Natalie Tennant]] declined to seek the nomination.  On May 10, 2016, Justice won the Democratic primary and became the nominee.<ref>{{cite news |last=Raby |first=John |url=http://www.herald-dispatch.com/elections/jim-justice-wins-democratic-nomination-for-west-virginia-gov/article_4feb8f22-9ea2-5ed3-bf31-4b887b799b8c.html |title=Jim Justice wins Democratic nomination for West Virginia gov |work=[[The Herald-Dispatch]] |date=May 10, 2015 |access-date=October 5, 2016 }}{{Dead link|date=January 2022 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

President of the Senate [[Bill Cole (West Virginia politician)|Bill Cole]], college student and former candidate for Mayor of [[Pineville, West Virginia|Pineville]] Andrew Utterback, and former [[Bramwell, West Virginia|Bramwell]] Police Chief and former Democratic candidate for House of Delegates Edwin Vanover ran for the Republican nomination. U.S. Representatives [[David McKinley]] and [[Evan Jenkins (politician)|Evan Jenkins]] declined to run for governor. [[List of West Virginia Attorneys General|West Virginia Attorney General]] [[Patrick Morrisey]] had been considered a potential Republican candidate, but instead chose to run for re-election. Potential Republican candidates included State Delegate [[Erikka Storch]] and Olympic gymnast [[Mary Lou Retton]].<ref name=FTWV>{{cite news|title=West Virginia Undergoing Political, Generational Change|url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/west_virginia_undergoing_political_generational_change-232603-1.html?pos=hln|access-date=May 1, 2014|newspaper=Roll Call|date=May 1, 2014}}</ref> Cole won the Republican nomination.

Justice won the election, taking 49.1% of the vote. Cole won 42.3%, while [[Charlotte Pritt]] of the [[Mountain Party]] won 5.9% of the vote.<ref name="politicoresults"/> Just months after assuming office, Justice switched to the Republican Party.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/maxjedeurpalmgren/2017/08/04/billionaire-and-wv-governor-jim-justice-switches-to-republican-at-trump-event/#51c8a568483b|title = Billionaire and WV Governor Jim Justice Switches to Republican at Trump Event}}</ref>

